      "content": "Wait, so you're saying it's \"rarely accidental\"? That's a huge deal. I knew it felt predatory, but hearing a professional say it actually hijacks peripheral vision... that makes it sound intentional. If they're using it to manufacture urgency/overwhelm, how deep does this go? Is this just a standard marketing trick or is there a more systematic way they're using these specific \"clashing patterns\" to bypass our actual decision-making processes? I'm trying to figure out if this is just bad design or if they've actually engineered this to manipulate us at a neurological level.",
